"Togolese politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Kwesi_Ahoomey-Zunu_(cropped).jpg/330px-Kwesi_Ahoomey-Zunu_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1958-12-01","died":null,"birthplace":"Lomé","parties":["Patriotic Pan-African Convergence"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Kwesi Ahoomey-Zunu"},"Q6428317":{"id":"Q6428317","name":"Komi Klassou","desc":"Togolese politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Komi_S%C3%A9lom_Klassou_24_mars_2017_(cropped).jpg/330px-Komi_S%C3%A9lom_Klassou_24_mars_2017_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1960-02-10","died":null,"birthplace":"Notsé","parties":["Rally of the Togolese People"],"education":["University of Lomé"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Komi Sélom Klassou"},"Q16682496":{"id":"Q16682496","name":"Victoire Dogbé Tomegah","desc":"Prime Minister of Togo (2020-2025)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Victoire_Tom%C3%A9gah-Dogb%C3%A9_-_P060895-274846.jpg/330px-Victoire_Tom%C3%A9gah-Dogb%C3%A9_-_P060895-274846.jpg","gender":"F","born":"1959-12-23","died":null,"birthplace":"Badougbe","parties":["Union for the Republic"],"education":["University of Lomé","Aarhus University","International Institute for Management Development"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Victoire Tomegah Dogbé"},"Q6169516":{"id":"Q6169516","name":"Jean-Lucien Savi de Tové","desc":"Togolese politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Amtseinf%C3%BChrung_Konsul_von_Togo_(cropped).jpg/330px-Amtseinf%C3%BChrung_Konsul_von_Togo_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1939-05-07","died":null,"birthplace":"Lomé","parties":["Patriotic Pan-African Convergence"],"education":["Institut d'études politiques de Bordeaux","Sorbonne University"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Jean-Lucien Savi de Tové"}},"summary":"Togo, officially the Togolese Republic, is a country located in West Africa. It is bordered by Ghana to the west, Benin to the east and Burkina Faso to the north. It is one of the least developed countries in the world and has a lower-middle income economy. It extends north from the Gulf of Guinea (where its capital, Lomé, is located) to the Burkina Faso–Togo border. It is a small, tropical country, spanning 57,000 square kilometres (22,000 square miles) with a population of approximately 8 million. It has a width of less than 115 km (71 mi) between Ghana and its eastern neighbour Benin.\nVarious people settled the boundaries of present-day Togo between the 11th and 16th centuries. Between the 16th and 18th centuries, the coastal region served primarily as a European slave trading outpost, earning Togo and the surrounding region the name \"The Slave Coast\". In 1884, during the scramble for Africa, Germany established a protectorate in the region called Togoland. After World War I, Togo was transferred to France with its contemporary borders. Togo gained independence from France in 1960. In 1967, Gnassingbé Eyadéma led a successful military coup d'état and became president of an anti-communist, single-party state. In 1993, Eyadéma faced multiparty elections marred by irregularities, and won the presidency three times. At the time of his death, Eyadéma was the \"longest-serving leader in modern African history\", having been president for 38 years. In 2005, his son Faure Gnassingbé was elected president.\nTogo is a tropical, sub-Saharan nation whose economy depends mostly on agriculture. The official language is French, while the two national languages are Kabiye and Ewe. Other languages are also spoken, particularly those of the Gbe family. Christians represent roughly half of the population (49.2%), followed by Muslims and adherents of traditional religions. Togo is a member of the United Nations, African Union, Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, South Atlantic Peace and Cooperation Zone, Francophonie, Commonwealth of Nations, and Economic Community of West African States.","history":"The history of Togo can be traced to archaeological finds which indicate that ancient tribes were able to produce pottery and process tin. During the period from the 11th century to the 16th century, the Ewé, the Mina, the Gun, and various other tribes entered the region. Most of them settled in coastal areas. The Portuguese arrived in the late 15th century, followed by other European powers. Until the 19th century, the coastal region was a major slave trade centre, earning Togo and the surrounding region the name \"The Slave Coast\".\nIn 1884, Germany claimed a coastal protectorate, which grew inland until it became the German colony of Togoland in 1905. A railway, the port of Lomé, and other infrastructure were developed. During the First World War, Togoland was invaded by Britain and France. In 1922, Great Britain received the League of Nations mandate to govern the western part of Togo and France to govern the eastern part. After World War II, these mandates became UN Trust Territories. The residents of British Togoland voted to join the Gold Coast as part of the new independent nation of Ghana in 1957.\nFrench Togoland became the Togolese Republic in 1960. Its Constitution, adopted in 1961, instituted the National Assembly of Togo as the supreme legislative body. In the same year, the first president, Sylvanus Olympio, dissolved the opposition parties and arrested their leaders. When he was assassinated in a coup in 1963, the military handed over power to an interim government led by Nicolas Grunitzky.\nThe military leader Gnassingbé Eyadéma overthrew Grunitzky in a bloodless coup in 1967. He assumed the presidency and introduced a one-party system in 1969. Eyadéma remained in power for the next 38 years. When he died in 2005, the military installed his son, Faure Gnassingbé, as president. Gnassingbe held elections and won, but the opposition claimed fraud. Because of political violence, around 40,000 Togolese fled to neighboring countries. Gnassingbé was re-elected two more times. In late 2017, anti-government protests were suppressed by security forces.","historyTitle":"History of Togo","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,1673110],[1961,1702720],[1962,1732960],[1963,1765070],[1964,1799410],[1965,1835590],[1966,1873490],[1967,1912760],[1968,1953520],[1969,2043420],[1970,2149480],[1971,2219830],[1972,2283860],[1973,2344510],[1974,2404290],[1975,2463800],[1976,2523490],[1977,2583980],[1978,2646500],[1979,2714350],[1980,2791410],[1981,2879440],[1982,2961650],[1983,3033080],[1984,3105740],[1985,3194540],[1986,3296340],[1987,3397610],[1988,3501550],[1989,3606920],[1990,3714020],[1991,3822640],[1992,3932580],[1993,3887970],[1994,3904180],[1995,4110450],[1996,4291140],[1997,4438790],[1998,4551640],[1999,4665750],[2000,4783720],[2001,4904140],[2002,5027130],[2003,5154110],[2004,5281320],[2005,5413150],[2006,5569660],[2007,5748440],[2008,5936140],[2009,6130950],[2010,6325790],[2011,6500580],[2012,6653720],[2013,6798850],[2014,6937640],[2015,7071500],[2016,7200880],[2017,7324390],[2018,7458210],[2019,7619930],[2020,7788050],[2021,7936180],[2022,8068620],[2023,8223850],[2024,8406560],[2025,8591630]],"gdpPerCapita":[[1960,102.239],[1961,104.831],[1962,107.761],[1963,114.616],[1964,130.361],[1965,144.099],[1966,162.919],[1967,171.07],[1968,174.91],[1969,185.029],[1970,166.862],[1971,182.288],[1972,207.562],[1973,244.84],[1974,329.182],[1975,353.837],[1976,346.616],[1977,424.884],[1978,439.836],[1979,463.966],[1980,574.92],[1981,471.976],[1982,391.787],[1983,356.531],[1984,326.547],[1985,337.014],[1986,454.51],[1987,519.181],[1988,556.1],[1989,529.714],[1990,619.185],[1991,591.939],[1992,607.946],[1993,448.034],[1994,355.43],[1995,449.856],[1996,482.275],[1997,476.89],[1998,492.493],[1999,477.217],[2000,440.42],[2001,426.884],[2002,479.438],[2003,579.728],[2004,604.311],[2005,595.2],[2006,596.249],[2007,654.084],[2008,771.351],[2009,770.173],[2010,750.323],[20